    "westernContext": "Western psychology reads teleporting dreams as windows into how the mind processes the desire for rapid change and the tension between escape and confrontation. Each major school offers a distinct and illuminating angle.\n\nFrom a Freudian perspective, teleportation is a textbook case of wish fulfillment — the unmet longing for instant gratification expressed through magical thinking in the dream state. Teleporting away from danger reflects avoidance mechanisms around repressed conflicts; freely moving to desired places indicates libidinal energy concentrated intensely on a specific goal. Freud would see these as symbolic releases of impulses suppressed during waking life.\n\nJung's analytical psychology frames teleportation as a marker of rapid individuation — the lifelong process of becoming a more integrated self. Freely chosen teleportation reflects a strengthening connection with the Self and movement toward psychological wholeness. Involuntary, disorienting teleportation suggests that unconscious contents not yet brought into awareness are overwhelming the Ego. When teleportation connects to the Trickster archetype, it signals creative disruption and the potential for transformative innovation.\n\nModern cognitive psychology's continuity hypothesis holds that dream content mirrors waking life concerns and emotions. Teleporting dreams therefore project real-world situations demanding rapid change, or the psychological desire to bypass complex processes. Teleportation accompanied by positive emotions indicates resilience and adaptability; anxious teleportation is a stress-response signal worth taking seriously. Recurring teleportation dreams are particularly worth paying attention to — they suggest the underlying situation has not been resolved.",
