    "culturalContext": "In traditional Korean folk belief, animals were not mere creatures but spiritual beings connected to humans through past-life karmic bonds (인연). Approximately 32.5% of Korean folk tales feature animal characters playing vital roles, often depicted as benevolent spirits helping those who face injustice.\n\nWithin this cultural framework, rescuing an animal is not simply a good deed but an act aligned with the cosmic order of heaven and earth (천지의 도리). Combined with the Buddhist concept of 방생 (life-release — freeing captive creatures to accumulate spiritual merit), saving an animal is seen as generating profound virtue (공덕) that inevitably returns as blessing.\n\nThe deeply rooted Korean narrative tradition of '은혜 갚는 동물' (the grateful animal) — tales of the grateful magpie, the grateful toad, and the grateful tiger — reinforces the belief that an animal you save will later repay you with fortune or protection. This cultural inheritance is what makes the rescuing-animal dream such a powerful omen of future reward in the Korean interpretive tradition.",
